      "expected": "A fan controller should use one active MCU, or provide explicit isolation/selection if these are mutually exclusive assembly alternatives. Independently driven MCU pins should not be tied together.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(comp (ref \"U1\") (value \"ATtiny45-20P\"))"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(comp (ref \"U4\") (value \"ATtiny13V-10S\"))"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (name \"NET0003\") ... (node (ref \"U1\") (pin \"1\")) (node (ref \"U4\") (pin \"1\")))"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (name \"NET0006\") ... (node (ref \"U1\") (pin \"6\")) (node (ref \"U4\") (pin \"6\")))"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (name \"NET0001\") ... (node (ref \"U1\") (pin \"7\")) (node (ref \"U4\") (pin \"7\")))"
        },
        {
          "source": "spec",
          "quote": "an ATtiny-driven fan controller"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "If both MCUs are assembled, their GPIO outputs can contend, causing incorrect operation or potentially excessive output current. If they are intended as alternatives, the files show no selection or isolation mechanism.",
      "confidence": 0.94
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The netlist places two different ATtiny MCUs on the same power rails and directly ties corresponding reset and GPIO pins together. This is unsafe if both are populated and lacks an explicit mechanism for choosing between them."
}

